WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.291 --> 00:00:01.835 align:start size:66% position:34% line:14%
Say that. Osda hniwi.

00:00:02.043 --> 00:00:03.586
Osda hniwi.

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:06.047
Hi. I’m Patrick Del Percio
and I’m a lecturer

00:00:06.047 --> 00:00:06.923
of Cherokee language

00:00:06.923 --> 00:00:08.258
in the Department of
Linguistics

00:00:08.258 --> 00:00:10.385 align:start position:0%
Now, if I want to add
some more to it

00:00:10.385 --> 00:00:12.721 align:start position:0%
or more emphasis, I'd say
Vsgidv.

00:00:12.887 --> 00:00:14.973
The big thing that’s hard
for people to get

00:00:14.973 --> 00:00:16.850
is the tone and the rhythm.

00:00:17.058 --> 00:00:19.185 align:end position:100%
Tohiquu.
Wait. [adjusts tone] Tohiquu.

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:21.813
The one word that comes to mind
is usdi.

00:00:21.813 --> 00:00:23.022
and usdi.

00:00:23.148 --> 00:00:26.401 line:76%
The first word is baby.
Which is usdi.

00:00:27.235 --> 00:00:29.237 line:76%
The second word is
[different tone] usdi.

00:00:29.237 --> 00:00:30.238
Which is small.

00:00:30.238 --> 00:00:34.409
So you can hear that slight
change or that slight tone,

00:00:34.409 --> 00:00:39.414
even the length of the
syllable, changes the meaning.

00:00:39.414 --> 00:00:41.833 align:start position:0%
So if you say Tla

00:00:42.292 --> 00:00:45.170
I’ve often heard other language
learners say that learning

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:47.297
the language changes
their world view.

00:00:47.297 --> 00:00:49.174
I hope to see that
from my students.

00:00:49.174 --> 00:00:50.633 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i>[students enjoying class]</i>

00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:53.553
I hope that heritage language
learners feel more connected

00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:58.308
to their heritage, their
ancestry, their identity.

00:00:58.558 --> 00:01:02.645
My late mentor, Durbin Feeling,
said that he envisioned

00:01:02.645 --> 00:01:04.272
a Cherokee renaissance.

00:01:04.272 --> 00:01:06.816
I hope that this is a
part of that.

